{
 "_": "Per-package test-duration budget for the CI timing gate (kata b6rv, see docs/developing-evener/testing.md). A Go package's value is its own wall time, read from the package-level terminal event of go test -json (issue #172) on the same non-fuzz gate surface run-module-tests.sh proves; for the frontend, the vitest JSON reporter's assertion durations rolled up under the single key \"web\". scripts/gate/test-timing-budget.sh compares a fresh measurement against these numbers: fail at 1.5x the budget, warn at 1.1x (strict in CI, warn-only on a local run) plus a per-test ceiling: strict by class for a test named in perTestCeilingClasses, warn-only flat for a test in no class (#4601). The top-level \"metric\" marker is what keeps the per-package ratios enforced; without it every ratio downgrades to a warning. Each Go package's number is the slowest wall time seen across recent CI runs of the tests lanes on main, floored at 2s so sub-second packages do not trip the ratio on runner noise; \"web\" is the slowest web-unit run over the same window. Raise a budget by editing this file in the same commit as the change that earned the increase, or run `make test-rebaseline` on a CI-class host to reset every listed package to what a clean run just measured. A package whose tests shell out to go build (cmd/evener-dev cross-compiles evener for windows; the mcp-server example builds itself) is budgeted at its cold build-cache time, because a change under shared code makes every queue candidate pay it: cmd/evener-dev reads 49s warm and 143-147s cold. Emptying \"packages\" puts the ratchet back in its no-baseline state, where every package reads as having no budget yet and the gate stays warn-only everywhere, CI included.",
 "perTestCeilingSeconds": 3,
 "_perTestCeilingClasses": "Per-test ceilings segmented by test class (#4601, Jesse's ruling 2026-10-13). A test whose name is listed in a class - or a subtest of one (Name/sub) - is compared STRICTLY against that class's ceiling, and a breach FAILs the check under --check in a CI-shaped environment or --strict. A test named in no class keeps the flat perTestCeilingSeconds above and stays warn-only in every mode, --strict included, so the unclassified backlog cannot red the gate. ceilingSeconds = the union of the class's largest CI observations across run 38458180180 and run 38468494246 (jobs tests / root, tests / nonroot, web-unit) + 25%, rounded up to a multiple of five seconds (#4601). The first run set the original ceilings; the first strict run (the second) breached install, transcript large-fixture and retirement, and brought live-stack E2E and dev-tool compile/CLI within 12% of theirs, so those five are the two runs' union max + 25%. A 2026-10-14 ruling (issue #4615) removed four runner-bound classes whose wall time measures the runner, not the code - dev-tool compile/CLI, install, transcript large-fixture and gate-runner integration - so their members now keep the flat perTestCeilingSeconds above and stay warn-only in every mode, like the rest of the unclassified backlog; the sighting was #4615's TestCrossCompileWindows at 52.26s against its 15s class ceiling on a 67-line diff in internal/transcriptindex that could not cost 37 seconds. A 2026-10-16 follow-up (issue #4699) moved TestLoadSessionJobActivityTree_TuesdayFullyFoldsAndFullyPaginates out of session-activity and onto the same warn-only default: it folds 200k events and pages through every one of them, and it runs t.Parallel() in the agent package, so its wall time tracks that package's parallel load rather than its own code - measured alone it takes 7.6-9.6s on a workstation at load 17, while CI read 30.09-32.28s for the same code, and the agent package gained 174 test declarations in the days after this class was blessed. The class keeps its other two members, which stay strict. Two further classes are deliberately absent: bounded-wait unit gets no ceiling of its own yet (a wall ceiling there mostly measures the runner, so its members stay unclassified and warn-only on the flat 3s), and the web repo-audit test was a class of one, split into one case per slice of the tracked TypeScript test files (#4608) rather than blessed. A 2026-10-27 follow-up moved TestE2E_RetireOverlappedByAnotherOwnershipAction off live-stack E2E onto the same warn-only default: it starts one hub-and-daemon stack per subtest, so its wall time is the sum of three runner-bound stack starts (6.8s typical, 15.3-16.9s on a loaded runner across six runs) and sat on either side of the 15s ceiling; each subtest alone is 2-6s. A test may be listed in only ONE class; a name matched by two classes is a config error the check FAILs on.",
